# Baseline, first so every later line can override it: text is stored LF and checked out LF
# on every machine, whatever core.autocrlf says. Without it a Windows checkout writes CRLF,
# and any check that reads checked-out bytes disagrees with the Linux CI runner about the
# same unchanged file. The `-text` lines below override it for evidence, whose bytes must
# not be converted in either direction.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The frozen corpus is EVIDENCE. Its bytes must survive a checkout unchanged, or
# the SHA-256 recorded at capture time stops matching the file it names — and an
# integrity record that cannot be checked is worse than none.
#
# Git's default on Windows converts LF to CRLF on checkout. That would rewrite
# every captured HTML body and every headers file. `-text` disables all EOL
# conversion for this directory, so what was captured is what is restored.
case-study-01/corpus/** -text

# The exhibits are evidence too, and the same rule applies for the same reason: a
# SHA-256 taken at freeze time must still match after a checkout. This covers the
# source files read out of other repositories AND the built artefact, which is a
# rendered HTML file whose whitespace IS the exhibit — EOL conversion would edit
# the very bytes the defect lives in.
case-study-01/exhibits/** -text

# The compressed bodies behind items 11, 42 and 48 (runs/crawl/bodies-*.jsonl.br).
# Binary brotli: a single converted byte would corrupt the stream and every
# body in it, and the hashes of 394 observations would stop being checkable.
runs/crawl/*.br -text

# The owner's verification screenshots behind item 56's tick. Binary PNGs, pinned by
# git blob; no conversion may ever touch them.
runs/owner-verification/** -text

# ALL run evidence, not two paths out of four. runs/ holds the raw observations, findings,
# census and export evidence that ticks stand on. runs/crawl/*.br and
# runs/owner-verification/** were already protected; the other 69 tracked files under runs/
# were not, and a Windows checkout rewrote every one of them on 13 September 2026.
# test/renderer.test.mjs:266 is the alarm that caught it; test/evidence-eol.test.mjs now
# holds the whole class, and was seen RED with this line removed.
runs/** -text

# F08's audit trail (22 September 2026). Same rule, same reason: every event carries an eventHash over its own
# canonical bytes and the previous event's hash, so a single EOL conversion on checkout would break the chain and
# the head record at once — and a tamper-evidence record that cannot be checked is worse than none.
#
# 🔴 IT IS NOT UNDER runs/ ON PURPOSE. Row 60's ruling sheet enumerates every .jsonl under runs/ as part of the
# issue and evidence corpus, so a trail kept there became an input to another feature's derived artefact and left it
# stale on every append. The suite caught that, and the store moved rather than the other feature's artefact being
# regenerated to absorb it. config/audit-store.mjs records the decision.
audit-trail/** -text
